WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
a lot has changed in the open world  genre since the saints row series came  to its ridiculous conclusion amid an  alien invasion almost 10 years ago and  with the reboot of the franchise deep  silver volition seems to be taking a  look back at that time before open world  games were flooded with rpg inspired  elements and crafting systems the new  saints row feels a lot like a game from  a different era but with a bunch of  additions that make it more than just a  nostalgic return to the past these guys  won't freaking die  we played about four hours of saints row  at a recent preview event which  encompassed the beginning of the game as  with the original saints row the new  game is all about creating your own  criminal empire but you have a long way  to go before that happens it's set in  the new city of santo elaso a fictional  location in the united states southwest  that mixes an urban center suburbs and  desert locations and carries the vibe of  places such as las vegas or albuquerque  without being quite like either one  though we know you're eventually going  to become a crime kingpin the game  starts with your humble beginnings where  your character is living in a dingy  apartment with three friends mechanic  and driving wiz nina business expert eli  and shirtless dj and resident cook kev  everyone in the group is familiar with  petty crime and both nina and kev are  members of local gangs nina's with los  panteros a gang that's focused on cars  and weightlifting while kev hangs around  with the idols a band of techno  anarchists who believe in a  decentralized future you're a member of  the third gang in town and the only  semi-legitimate one a private military  corporation called martial defense  industries so at the start of saints row  your goal is to climb the corporate  ladder as a mercenary at marshall mostly  focused on bringing home enough rent  money to keep you and your friends from  eviction trying to find a way to pull  yourself up by your bootstraps and fight  an economic system stacked against you  is a pretty pervasive theme in saints  row at one point nina mentions the  driving force for her doing crimes at  all is to earn money to pay off her  student loans  your character is already a talented  killer full of bravado so the  opportunity to earn bonus pay for  performing well incentivizes you to  become a one-person maverick murder  machine  [ __ ]  take the turret i got it i'm good with  these the opening mission is your first  day with marshall dropping you and a  bunch of other soldiers into an  abandoned old west town filled with los  panteros gangsters guarding their boss  the mission highlights the lessons  saints row has taken from modern open  world games  it's a pretty cinematic event with  cutscenes popping up here and there as  you fight through waves of enemies and  advance on your objective  this approach gives those missions the  kind of storytelling gravitas you often  get out of more linear single-player  games and were reminiscent of what you  might see in the horizon games for  instance with a lot of emphasis placed  on characters and action driven scenes  in terms of gameplay however saints row  often feels like a throwback  the third person shooting aspect of the  game is pretty standard but i had a  really tough time getting used to the  feeling of aiming in saints row  the controller analog sticks were way  too sensitive with seemingly no way to  adjust them it took me a bit to get the  hang of how to hit targets which is best  done by making liberal use of the auto  aim assistance you get from aiming down  sights which magnetizes your reticle  onto a foe it reminded me of old school  open world shooting from the height of  saints row or the grand theft auto  series but not really in a good way  while the shooting itself is a bit  finicky saints row introduces cool  additions to gameplay that updated  overall  your health bar is divided into three  parts and while your health will  automatically recharge if you're out of  the line of fire for a few seconds it  won't refill fully so if you're between  one-third health and two-thirds health  you'll only recharge to the two-thirds  mark to get your health back fully you  have to kill enemies using spiffy  cinematic execution moves that you can  activate with a single button press when  you're close enough  the trouble is the execution ability  also charges over time so you can't  always pull one off  kills recharge the ability more quickly  so the more mayhem you create the more  executions you can pull off and the more  you can heal yourself it's a healing  system reminiscent of something like do  maternal encouraging you to constantly  mix it up in combat rather than hunker  down behind cover the first marshall  mission ends with you taking down the  bad guy against all odds including  stopping him from flying away in a jet  and fighting him on top of the plane  unfortunately disobeying orders and  taking matters into your own hands  doesn't earn you the acknowledgement or  bonus pay you'd hope for completing  missions earns you experience points  that play into a leveling progression  system the fruits of which make you more  of an overall badass leveling up takes  you along a linear progression path  unlocking either new equippable skills  or earning you passive upgrades like  boost to your overall health the skills  are the cool part as you can assign them  to certain buttons and use them in the  heat of battle the first one you unlock  allows you to grab a nearby enemy shove  a grenade down their pants and then  heave them back toward their friends and  it's incredibly useful for crowd control  the next main story mission your second  day on the job introduces some of saints  row's car combat elements sending you on  a mission to follow a panteros convoy  that quickly degrades into an all-out  battle of course cars are a big part of  saints row and you'll do a fair amount  of driving as well as shooting while  driving you can shoot out of the windows  when you're driving or riding in a car  but you're relegated to small guns and a  limited range of motion when you do so  to be more effective and deadly you can  climb on top of the car allowing you to  fire in all directions with bigger guns  but on the roof you have zero cover and  thus take a lot more damage from enemies  shooting back at you  after what feels like years of drive-by  shootings in games like this the ability  to climb in and out of cars to fight  during chases makes them much more  dynamic giving you some tactical options  and trade-offs to consider  by your third day on the job your  corporate overlords seem to have taken a  shine to you offering you a chance to  run security at a museum while a special  object called the codex is displayed the  other gangs show up to try to steal it  and despite the fact that you kill a  ridiculous number of them the codex is  ultimately swiped you're fired in  spectacular fashion despite heroic  efforts to save at least one life along  the way another reminder that the system  doesn't really care how hard you work  that codex also seems like it'll be an  important part to the story later on  although it's tough to see how at this  point  the best part of the story i've seen so  far is the dynamic between the main  characters the small group of friends  has an undeniable chemistry and their  commitment to one another provides the  drive to the story of founding the  saints they're fun interesting  characters and seeing how they develop  is as much a reason to keep playing as  shooting people and stealing cars as an  investor i don't like wasting money  you're wearing a [ __ ] bow tie  okay i will throw in 20 bucks for waffle  maker saints row also adopts something  of a different tone from the series past  it's much more grounded than the alien  invasion superhero story of saints row 4  but maintains major action comedy vibes  it's just more bad boys than the suicide  squad the whole thing is still extremely  over the top with plenty of headshots  and explosions this is certainly not a  serious game saints row puts its comedic  focus on its characters personalities  and on satirizing things like heartless  corporations and cryptocurrency  enthusiasts who want to destroy society  when you're not working on saints row's  main story there are plenty of other  side hustle missions popping up around  the world that allow you to earn more  money to buy guns cars and clothes for  later on these tend toward the goofy  side of the spectrum as well saints row  staples like insurance fraud make a  return in which you jump in front of  cars and otherwise injure yourself for  cash while other side hustles have you  flying in your new wingsuit or posting  really bad reviews about restaurants to  draw out and eliminate their killer  backers  there are also a ton of gameplay  challenges you can complete as you play  like driving in the oncoming traffic  lane for a certain amount of time or  killing a set number of enemies with  headshots and many of these earn you  special passive traits called perks that  you can equip to your character the perk  and leveling systems that unlock new  skills feel like another way volition is  merging the sort of old-school open  world chaos a game like saints row is  good at with more modern ideas of  character customization and creating  loadouts challenges are something you  complete while you're messing around in  the world or doing other missions but  the fact that you can change skills and  perks whenever you want means you're  encouraged to think strategically about  your abilities  the best part of saints row are the  combination of new ideas that play into  the strengths of this kind of open world  game where unpredictable fights with  gangs on the street or running from the  cops prove just as fun and rewarding as  the story though the game does suffer  some of the drawbacks of this style of  open world game like weird ai and weak  shooting a hearty helping of hilarious  unpredictability funny writing and  strong characters seem like they'll  provide lots of reasons to return to  saints row  you [ __ ] lied to us

